        {
          "type": "code",
          "say": "In code, apply does both jobs: it fixes the node's sum right away, and records the debt for its children. Push down hands the debt to both children, then clears it. Range add calls apply on fully covered nodes and stops. On a partial node, it pushes down first, so the children are current, then recurses, and re-merges. Query adds the same push down before splitting.",
          "props": {
            "title": "apply and <em>pushDown</em>",
            "file": "lazy.cpp",
            "tag": "C++",
            "lang": "cpp",
            "size": 20,
            "width": 1250,
            "code": "void apply(int node, int lo, int hi, long long d) {\n    t[node] += d * (hi - lo + 1);    // the sum is right now\n    lazy[node] += d;                 // the children owe d each\n}\nvoid pushDown(int node, int lo, int hi) {\n    if (lazy[node] == 0) return;\n    int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;\n    apply(2 * node, lo, mid, lazy[node]);\n    apply(2 * node + 1, mid + 1, hi, lazy[node]);\n    lazy[node] = 0;\n}\nvoid rangeAdd(int node, int lo, int hi, int l, int r, long long d) {\n    if (r < lo || hi < l) return;\n    if (l <= lo && hi <= r) { apply(node, lo, hi, d); return; }\n    pushDown(node, lo, hi);          // before touching the children\n    int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;\n    rangeAdd(2 * node, lo, mid, l, r, d);\n    rangeAdd(2 * node + 1, mid + 1, hi, l, r, d);\n    t[node] = t[2 * node] + t[2 * node + 1];\n}\n// query(): the same as before, plus pushDown() before it splits",
